      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Arthroscopic decompression (wafer procedure) of the ulnocarpal compartment in patients with ulnar impaction syndrome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INDICATIONS",
          "content": "The arthroscopic wafer procedure is recommended in ulnar-plus situations with up to 3 mm length-excess. To perform this procedure the ulnar head needs to be accessible for the burr through a pre-existing, impaction-related, centroradial lesion of the triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC). The additional presence of a distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ) type C confirms the indication."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "content": "The wafer procedure is contraindicated if there is no consistent TFCC injury ensuring access to the ulnar head and furthermore in ulna-plus situations of more than 3 mm. Relative contraindications: in young patients due to lack of evidence-based studies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SURGICAL TECHNIQUE",
          "content": "Arthroscopic, semicircular, partial resection of the ulnar head in terms of oblique-helicoidal osteotomy using a 4.2 mm burr, while sparing the DRUJ and the dorsal and the palmar radioulnar ligaments."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "POSTOPERATIVE MANAGEMENT",
          "content": "Immobilization for 1 week in a palmar splint with immediate intensive exercising of pro- and supination under physiotherapeutic instruction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Between 2008 and 2010, an arthroscopic wafer procedure was performed in 24 patients. The resection of the ulnar head was 2.5 mm on average. After a mean follow-up time of 13.25 months, very good results were archived in 23 of 24 patients; the ulnar impingement test was negative. On a visual analog scale (0-10) average postoperative pain was 1.16 at rest and 4.5 under stress. The mean postoperative DASH score was 13.4."
        }
      ]},
